# Line endings are content here, so git is told rather than asked.
#
# Without a rule, core.autocrlf decides, and it is a per-machine setting:
# a Windows checkout produced CRLF, a Linux one LF, and the two disagreed
# about the contents of every multi-line Turtle literal, because a CRLF
# inside one is part of that literal's value. Measured over the whole
# corpus that is 77 literal-object triples in 8 files -- see
# config/eol-transition-matrix.json, line_ending_transition.
#
# 11 of those 77 are sh:select: SPARQL text a validator executes, not
# prose somebody reads. The rest are bibRef, rdfs:comment, skos notes and
# owl:versionInfo. So this is not a cosmetic difference in annotations.
#
# The rule is repository-wide and not limited to *.ttl, because the
# evidence has the same problem as the corpus. config/semantic-baseline.json
# records the sha256 of the matrix artifact beside it; with only *.ttl
# pinned, a fresh checkout re-encoded that JSON to CRLF and the recorded
# digest could not match the bytes on disk anywhere except the machine
# that wrote it. A byte digest over a file git is free to re-encode is
# not a byte digest. This was found by checking out the commit that
# introduced the fix and running the suite against it.
#
# The index is already LF throughout (327 text blobs at the time of
# writing), so this pins existing behaviour rather than changing it.
#
# The byte digests recorded before this rule describe the machine that
# measured them.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Redundant under the rule above, and kept anyway: the corpus is the
# reason the rule exists, and a future narrowing of `*` should not
# silently take the corpus with it.
*.ttl text eol=lf

# Detected as binary by content today; declared so the detection is not
# what stands between an archive and a corrupting newline translation.
*.zip -text
*.png -text
.DS_Store -text
